@cc [owner:jchen0824,label:security;api] connectors-api-enabled-provider-guard
When `CONNECTORS_ENABLED_PROVIDERS` is set, a handler that creates a connector, or that can start
work for a connector or provider (a workflow, a sync, a provider-side write), MUST refuse every
provider the list does not enable before any side effect for it: no connector row, no OAuth or
credential call, no workflow. It checks through `checkConnectorProviderEnabled`,
`withEnabledConnectorProviders` or `checkResolvedConnectorProvidersEnabled`, or through a lookup
that returns only connectors of enabled types, such as
`SlackConfigurationResource.fetchByActiveBot`. A provider-specific route checks its own provider.
Every connector that the handler, or the code it calls, can start work for MUST be checked by that
connector's own type where it is resolved, unless that lookup can only return connectors of a type
already checked: it filters on that type, or reads a table that only that provider writes (Slack's
configuration table is written by both `slack` and `slack_bot`). A check of the route's provider,
of an admin major command or of another connector does not cover any other connector. This
includes a connector named in the request body, the team's active Slack bot, and a connector looked
up from the one the request names. When such a looked-up connector is refused, the handler starts
no work for it and goes on with the rest of the request. A workflow that the handler starts applies
the same checks in the worker, with the worker's own `CONNECTORS_ENABLED_PROVIDERS`. A handler
whose request can act on several providers or connectors, such as `POST /connectors/admin`, checks
every one of them and refuses a request whose providers it cannot resolve. Pausing, deleting and
read-only handlers stay available for every provider. When the variable is unset, handlers behave
as upstream.

@cc [owner:jchen0824,label:security;testing] connectors-api-enabled-provider-guard-coverage
`enabled_connector_providers.test.ts` MUST list every route that `startServer` registers, in
`GUARDED_ROUTES` or in `UNGUARDED_ROUTES`. A route whose handler is guarded by
`checkConnectorProviderEnabled`, `withEnabledConnectorProviders` or
`checkResolvedConnectorProvidersEnabled` MUST be in `GUARDED_ROUTES`, sent through the app
`startServer` builds, with a request that its guard refuses with 403 and no side effect when the
list does not enable the route's provider, and that reaches the handler when the variable is
unset. The test MUST also check that `ADMIN_COMMAND_TARGETS` classifies exactly the commands of
`AdminCommandSchema`, and, for each connector that a guarded handler resolves other than by the
route's own check (a body connector id, the active Slack bot, a looked-up connector), that a list
not enabling that connector's type leaves it without work.
